  - CHANGELOG.md
decisions:
  - id: d-helper-location-premise-core
    decision: checkAspectAnchors + resolveAnchorPath live in premise-core, with a back-compat shim left in paradigm-mcp/src/utils/anchor-path.ts
    rationale: premise-core is the only package all 3 callers can import; the CLI cannot import paradigm-mcp. Injecting the resolver instead would recreate the divergence at the CLI site.
  - id: d-neutral-result-type
    decision: Helper returns neutral AspectAnchorIssue[] ({aspectSymbol, anchorRaw, kind}); each caller maps to its own output
    rationale: pm_postflight emits PostflightViolation, review emits Finding — keeping the helper output-agnostic prevents coupling and lets all 3 share one implementation.
learnings:
  - A bug fix that lands on only one of N duplicated copies is a latent re-report. When fixing a validator bug, grep for sibling re-implementations of the same logic BEFORE declaring done — here the same aspect-anchor check existed in 3 places (pm_postflight, paradigm review CLI, compliance-checker) and v6.6.2 fixed only one, so the false positive resurfaced from a different surface.
  - Cross-package shared helpers must live in the lowest common package both/all callers can import. The CLI (paradigm) cannot import paradigm-mcp, so injecting a resolver from paradigm-mcp would force the CLI to reimplement the buggy resolver — the consolidation had to move resolveAnchorPath + the helper down into premise-core.
  - A shared helper that feeds differently-shaped outputs (PostflightViolation vs Finding) should return a NEUTRAL result type each caller maps to its own shape — keeps formatting out of the helper and avoids coupling.
  - premise-core had no test infrastructure before this; lower-level core packages can silently lack vitest, so 'run the tests' there is a no-op until you add the config.
